    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Jerusalem’s first town hall opened in April 1902 near Yondota Center, marking the start of organized local government.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://twp.jerusalem.oh.us/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The township was officially formed on March 10, 1893, covering 34 square miles of land, 14 miles of shoreline, and 216 square miles of water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://twp.jerusalem.oh.us/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original town hall building still exists, having been relocated to a farm just outside the fire station after 1947.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://twp.jerusalem.oh.us/history/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Jerusalem lies along Jerusalem Road, designated State Route 2, which has long connected the village to the surrounding region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://twp.jerusalem.oh.us/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The village sits on the southwestern Lake Erie shoreline, where the living remnants of the Great Black Swamp can still be seen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://twp.jerusalem.oh.us/history/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Dark, rich soil turned agriculture into the backbone of Jerusalem’s early economy, sustaining the community for generations.",
